WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:02.610
The housing market is red-hot right now!

00:00:02.610 --> 00:00:03.860
So what were you thinking?

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:04.860
Three bedroom?

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:05.860
Four bedroom?

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:07.860
I think that might be a little bit above my budget.

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:09.860
Okay, so what were you thinking?

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:12.120
I'd love to keep it under a hundred.

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:13.260
$100,000?

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:15.100
Okay, I'll see what I can do.

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:17.580
Oh no, I meant a hundred dollars…

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:23.540
Wait, you’re hoping to invest in real estate
with only a hundred dollars?

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:24.600
That’s ridiculous!

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:26.640
Or is it???

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:39.820
Are you itching to get into the real estate
game, but lack the tens of thousands needed

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:41.510
for a basic down payment?

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:46.540
Or maybe you hate the idea of dragging yourself
to open houses comparing different backsplash

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:47.540
treatments.

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:51.850
Well, there’s a way to own real-estate without
investing in individual properties: buying

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:56.200
shares of a Real Estate Investment Trust,
or REIT for short.

00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:57.960
Let us give you a tour!

00:00:57.970 --> 00:01:03.200
A REIT is a company that owns and most of
the time operates income-producing real estate.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:06.719
They are a fairly new fixture in the investment
world.

00:01:06.719 --> 00:01:11.670
The very first REITs were created in the U.S.
in 1960 by Congress.

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:17.679
They were designed as a way for everyday people
to own a piece of a large real estate investment

00:01:17.679 --> 00:01:18.679
portfolio.

00:01:18.679 --> 00:01:23.959
Since then, they’ve exploded in popularity
with publicly available REITs in 35 countries

00:01:23.959 --> 00:01:26.479
valued at over a trillion dollars!

00:01:26.479 --> 00:01:28.140
And it’s easy to see why.

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:31.909
REITs are historically one of the best performing
asset classes.

00:01:31.909 --> 00:01:39.990
Between 1990 and 2010, the primary equity
REIT Index grew at 9.9% per year, second only

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:41.479
to mid-cap stocks.

00:01:41.479 --> 00:01:48.399
Also, here in America, REITs don’t have
to pay corporate income tax as long as 75%

00:01:48.399 --> 00:01:51.889
or more of the fund’s assets are invested
in real estate.

00:01:51.889 --> 00:01:56.399
Which means that the business overall has
a lot more wiggle room cash-flow wise.

00:01:56.399 --> 00:02:01.209
But the biggest draw for investors is the
income-generation.

00:02:01.209 --> 00:02:08.060
By law, REITs must pay at least 90% of taxable
income to investors in the form of dividends.

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:09.600
And they’re nothing to sniff at.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:15.280
The average equity REIT--one that buys properties--pays
a 5% annual dividend.

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:20.700
The average mortgage REIT--which owns mortgage
backed securities--pays a 10% dividend!

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:26.430
Compare that to the average S&amp;P 500 stock,
which pays 1.9%, and you can start to understand

00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:27.430
the curb appeal!

00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:33.310
While REITs are required to pay 90% of taxable
income out in dividends, most REITs actually

00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:37.290
pay more than 100% of income out to shareholders.

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:40.040
But that’s not as exciting as you might
think.

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:46.110
A hefty 10% pay out isn’t as attractive
if your share value falls by 15%.

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:49.800
That’s why it’s important to look at the
“total return”.

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:54.540
You can calculate your “total return”
by combining the dividend returns along with

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:56.660
the share price movement.

00:02:56.660 --> 00:03:01.790
When you think about it, these two levers
are very much like owning a physical investment

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:02.790
property.

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:06.510
On one hand, you are bringing in income from
the rent you charge your tenants.

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:10.240
Then there’s also the market value of the
home itself that should (hopefully) be going

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:11.800
up in value over time.

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:16.530
So even if you’re not making a ton of profit
through rent after paying a mortgage and taxes

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:21.320
and upkeep, you could be experiencing a return
through the appreciation of the property.

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:22.710
Or it could work vice versa.

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:25.900
And like property, REITs come in a lot of
different styles.

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:30.540
For example...Retail REITs invest in shopping
malls and retail outlets.

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:35.760
Residential REITs contain apartment complexes
and manufactured housing, and Office REITs

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:38.410
have...you guessed it...office building complexes.

00:03:38.410 --> 00:03:43.319
They can also focus on specific locations
-- maybe you’re interested in Manhattan

00:03:43.319 --> 00:03:48.010
real estate, or India’s first REIT that
just debuted in March of 2019?

00:03:48.010 --> 00:03:52.450
There are plenty of databases out there that
can help you narrow your search by focus,

00:03:52.450 --> 00:03:54.570
region, historical returns, and more.

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:58.600
The final consideration is whether to go public
or private.

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:03.360
Shares in private REITs are offered exclusively
through financial advisors and brokers, and

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:09.459
they can offer enticingly high dividends or
niche specialties like resorts, senior living

00:04:09.459 --> 00:04:11.630
centers or timber farms.

00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:15.510
But make sure you put on a hard hat when checking
them out.

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:19.690
Private REITs can pay extremely high commissions
to the brokers that sell them…sometimes

00:04:19.690 --> 00:04:21.799
up to 12% of your investment!

00:04:21.799 --> 00:04:26.639
They aren't required to disclose conflicts
of interest, and it's difficult to find performance

00:04:26.639 --> 00:04:28.419
data on private REITs as a whole.

00:04:28.419 --> 00:04:33.800
And they aren’t very liquid, so getting
your money back quickly can be difficult.

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:38.289
And they’re usually for accredited investors
only… meaning you have to have a million

00:04:38.289 --> 00:04:43.620
dollars to invest or have made over $200,000
for the last two years.

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:47.810
If that doesn’t sound like you, buying a
publicly traded REIT is as easy as buying

00:04:47.810 --> 00:04:49.069
a share of stock.

00:04:49.069 --> 00:04:52.659
You can log into an online brokerage account,
and just look up the ticker.

00:04:52.659 --> 00:04:57.099
And if you can’t decide on which REIT to
pick, there are even ETFs that own stock in

00:04:57.099 --> 00:05:01.349
different REIT companies -- kind of a sampler
platter of real estate holdings.

00:05:01.349 --> 00:05:06.120
You’re saying I can buy hundreds of different
pieces of real estate online, without comparing

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:10.430
square footage, worrying about old fixtures,
or getting calls from angry tenants in the

00:05:10.430 --> 00:05:11.939
middle of the night?

00:05:11.939 --> 00:05:12.939
Yep.

00:05:12.939 --> 00:05:17.490
But there’s something special about owning
your own property, taking care of it, living

00:05:17.490 --> 00:05:18.490
in it.

00:05:18.490 --> 00:05:21.960
Sure, but if you live in a city where property
values are inflated, or you don’t plan to

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:26.139
stay in one place for very long, REITs could
be a way to dip your toe in the real estate

00:05:26.140 --> 00:05:28.820
market for as little as a $100.

00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:30.100
And that’s our two cents!

